\title{expectr: pipeline checkpoints for data expectations}
\description{
Statistical production code lives or dies on small assumptions: no
duplicate units, no negative amounts, no person with two addresses at
once. expectr states each assumption as one line in the pipeline, a
\emph{checkpoint}, built on a single idea: express the rule as "the set of
violations must be empty", build that set with ordinary dplyr, and pass
it on. See \code{vignette("expectr")}.
}
\details{
\subsection{Where to start}{
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=expect_empty]{expect_empty()}}: the violation set you pipe in has no rows.
\item \code{\link[=expect_none]{expect_none()}}: no row matches a condition; returns the full data.
\item \code{\link[=expect_unique]{expect_unique()}}, \code{\link[=expect_balanced]{expect_balanced()}}, \code{\link[=expect_no_overlap]{expect_no_overlap()}}: the
register rules that are awkward to write as a \code{filter()}.
\item \code{\link[=tally_violations]{tally_violations()}}: counts violations for many rules at once, and
leaves the verdict to you.
\item \code{\link[=flag_duplicates]{flag_duplicates()}} and \code{\link[=view_types]{view_types()}}: inspect duplicates and column
types before you settle on a rule.
}
}

\subsection{Why a package of its own}{

The checkpoints started in the daos package. They moved here because
they share an engine and a contract the rest of daos does not: every
function either judges a violation set or helps build one, with the same
messages, logging, dumps and condition class, and the same grouping
engine for keys such as cpr. daos re-exports them for existing scripts.
}
